<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hanno the Navigator</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magonid-dynasty">Magonid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hanno the Navigator was a Carthaginian explorer who led a voyage along the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> around 500 BCE. An account of the voyage survives in a Greek version known as a periplus, and how far he reached remains debated. His expedition is counted among the achievements of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The name Hanno was given to many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a>, and ancient texts do not identify the navigator clearly. Some authors call him a king; others use the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> dux or imperator. The Greek version of his account calls him a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basileus">basileus</a>, a word also used for other high Carthaginian officials. Scholarly consensus places him in the fifth century BCE and identifies him as a member of the aristocratic Magonid family; R. C. C. Law takes him to be a son of Hamilcar I.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">gold trade</a> had been a vital foundation of Carthaginian power from the fifth century BCE, and the wish to secure the gold route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> may have been the original reason for exploring the coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> sent Hanno with a fleet of 60 ships to explore and colonise the north-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. He sailed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a> and founded or resettled seven colonies on the coast of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, then continued some distance south, meeting various peoples. The account gives 30,000 men for the expedition, a number some scholars question, suggesting about 5,000.</p><p>How far he went is disputed. Harden reports a consensus that he reached at least <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, with estimates of the distance ranging from under 1,100 to at least 4,800 kilometres, and others propose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a>. Raymond Mauny argued that ancient navigators could not have sailed much beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-bojador">Cape Bojador</a>, because winds and currents would hinder the return. The account ends with an island of hairy people called Gorillai by Hanno's interpreters; their skins were brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> says they survived until Carthage was destroyed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Hanno's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus</a> is one of the few surviving ancient accounts of exploration written by the explorer himself. The original, probably in Punic, is lost, and the surviving Greek abridgement of 101 lines exists in two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the 9th and 14th centuries. Conrad Gessner printed the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation at Zurich in 1559. Most attempts to locate the places it names from its sailing distances have failed, and some scholars suspect the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> altered it to protect their trade.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> both knew the text. Pliny claimed that Hanno sailed from Gades to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, which modern scholarship considers unrealistic. A lunar crater is named after Hanno. Interest in the voyage grew in the 16th century, when European exploration was flourishing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hanno the Navigator” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hanno the Navigator</b> sailed down the Atlantic coast of Africa, probably in the fifth century BCE, and may have reached as far as Sierra Leone or even Cameroon according to competing interpretations of his surviving periplus; it was the most ambitious known exploration of that coast before the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The word gorilla comes from Punic: <b>Hanno the Navigator</b>’s interpreters gave the name to a tribe of hostile ‘hairy people’ in the Gulf of Guinea, it passed into Greek as gorillai, and in 1847 Thomas S. Savage applied it to the western gorilla.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.85,10.31&amp;year=-500" aria-label="Carthage on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hanno the Navigator"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5702 4384h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5702 4384h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.85,10.31&amp;year=-500">What was here in 500 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
